# timecrate.exclude — junk to prune from inside the included paths. Installed to
# /etc/timecrate/exclude and fed to `tar --exclude-from`.
#
# tar matching (verified): a bare name (.cache) matches at ANY depth; a relative path
# (home/you/big/data) matches that exact subtree; never a leading slash; spaces are literal
# (no quoting in this file). `~/` expands to the configured user's home, as in the include list.
#
# NOTE: the decryption keyring, the rclone token and the staging directory are excluded
# automatically by the tool, from live config — they are not listed here and must not be
# re-added. Deleting a line from this file can only cost you disk, never your key.

# --- caches / build artifacts / VCS internals (match at any depth) ---
.cache
.npm
node_modules
__pycache__
.venv
.pytest_cache
.ruff_cache
.mypy_cache
.git
*.pyc
*.o
.DS_Store

# --- trash and thumbnails ---
~/.local/share/Trash
~/.thumbnails
~/.cache

# --- add your own bulk here, e.g. ---
#~/Downloads
#~/VirtualBox VMs

# --- giants inside the included trees -----------------------------------------------------------
# ~/.config is dominated by browser profiles (7.1G on the box this was measured on, of which 6.6G
# was Chrome and Brave). Excluding them keeps the settings tree around 7M. ~/.config/Code/User
# stays included — only caches are dropped, so VS Code settings survive.
~/.config/google-chrome
~/.config/google-chrome-headless
~/.config/BraveSoftware
~/.config/chromium
~/.config/vivaldi
~/.config/opera
~/.config/microsoft-edge
# Path-qualified, NOT bare names. `Cache`, `CachedData`, `blob_storage` and friends as unanchored
# patterns apply to the WHOLE archive, so any directory named Cache under /usr/local, /etc or a
# personal tree would be dropped silently — the exact class of quiet incompleteness the rest of
# this tool exists to prevent, and it would have been invisible because the backup still succeeds.
# Qualified this way the worst case is a slightly larger kit when an app adds a cache directory,
# which is a failure direction you can see.
~/.config/Code/Cache
~/.config/Code/CachedData
~/.config/Code/CachedExtensionVSIXs
~/.config/Code/CachedProfilesData
~/.config/Code/Code Cache
~/.config/Code/GPUCache
~/.config/Code/WebStorage
~/.config/Code/logs
~/.config/Code/blob_storage
~/.config/Code/Service Worker
~/.config/Code/Crashpad
